WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.640
When Beijing won the bid to host the 2008
Summer Olympics, China knew it had been handed

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.989
a once-in-history opportunity.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:41.719
What would’ve been a mere sporting event
for 3rd place Paris or 2nd place Toronto,

00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:46.410
was more akin to a national graduation ceremony
for China.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.440
As 30,000 fireworks exploded on the opening
day,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.610
As 10,000 performers performed,
And when the Olympic cauldron was finally

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:59.230
lit,
China’s symbolic tassel was turned, and

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:04.470
it ceased being, if only in our minds, just
another developing country.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.940
China was now a global player.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:13.190
While the Olympics always take on a kind of
performative quality, rarely are the stakes

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.820
so high.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.350
After all, a nation only graduates once.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:24.130
In the seven years leading up to the event,
Beijing underwent both a physical and cultural

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.770
transformation.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:31.490
Its airport received a $1 billion upgrade,
three new subway lines were built, and 23

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.500
new roads were laid.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:39.270
Campaigns were launched to fight against spitting,
smoking, littering, and cutting in line — anything

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.420
that might give visitors a negative impression.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.170
In the words of then-Premier Wen Jiabao, the
goal was to depict the country as “democratic,

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.570
open, civilized, friendly, and harmonious”.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:58.729
Humorous as this description may sound today,
it once wasn’t such a ridiculous idea.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:03.439
Having just joined the World Trade Organization,
and in the context of a thirty-year reform

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:09.450
era led by Deng Xiaoping, when a smiling Jackie
Chan sang “Beijing Welcomes You” in front

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:16.060
of the Great Wall, frankly, one was inclined
to believe him, however cheesy the music video.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.270
China was on its best behavior.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.420
A little bit of wishful thinking was warranted.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:27.480
A lot has changed since 2008.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:33.660
Gone are the days of people exchanges, Confucius
Institutes, and liberalization fantasies.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:39.930
In, are crackdowns, crude insults, explicit
threats, and war games.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:45.360
Today, it’s awfully hard to believe that
the China who “opened its arms to the world”

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.620
— who declared the “whole world” its
friend — is the same country we know now,

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.260
just ten short years apart.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:58.900
A decade ago, to observe that China sought
soft power would’ve seemed humorously banal

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.430
— what nation doesn’t?

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:09.049
The record-setting $100 million+ price tag
of the 2008 opening ceremony made very clear

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:12.830
exactly how much it cared what you thought
of it.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:18.170
2021 China, on the other hand, is liable to
do enough reputational damage on any given

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:23.190
Tuesday to offset that entire era of reputation
building.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.280
In an almost too-Hollywood ending, the lyricist
behind “Beijing Welcomes You” — a song

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:33.160
almost universally loved in China — would
eventually be erased from the Chinese internet

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.810
after showing support for Hong Kong protestors.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:42.669
What happened to make China aggressively and
needlessly burn bridges with the same impressive

00:03:42.669 --> 00:03:46.220
vigor it once made friends?

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.919
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the bonus video which accompanies

00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:54.060
this one.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:04:00.730
Around 2019 — long after every last grandma,
Luddite, puppy, and local car dealership finally

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:06.230
gave in and created a Twitter account — the
company found a surprising new source of user

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.660
growth: Chinese Diplomats.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:16.120
Embassies, consulates, politicians, and ambassadors
all suddenly and inexplicably decided to join

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.070
the site around the same time — naturally,
raising some eyebrows.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:27.790
After years of largely ignoring American social
media, what new 280-character insights did

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.889
they just have to share with the world?

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:33.050
Not much, it turns out.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.770
Follow one of these accounts and you’d see
a continuous stream of Xi Jinping smiling

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:42.040
at various objects, among other mundane headlines.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:48.050
Top hashtags used by these accounts included
captivating topics like the #China-Pakistan

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.389
Economic Corridor.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:55.669
Once a year you’d be blessed with a flurry
of posts about #TwoSessions — a reference

00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:59.610
to the annual plenary sessions of the National
People's Congress.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:05.949
Clearly, the Communist Party was still a long
way from mastering the art of subtweeting.

00:05:05.949 --> 00:05:12.069
Interspersed with these glorified press releases,
however, was, every once in a while, something

00:05:12.069 --> 00:05:13.830
very different.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:20.020
The Chinese embassy in Brazil accused its
president of having a “mental virus”,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:26.610
Another diplomat addressed the Prime Minister
of Canada as “boy” and called him a “spendthrift”.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.889
These are two of the more tame examples.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:35.610
Members of the Chinese Foreign Ministry have
hurled such bizarre insults as “crazed hyena”,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.919
“fatso”, and “running dog” — and
at citizens of their host countries, no less.

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:46.520
They’ve promoted conspiracy theories, spread
offensive images, and mocked India’s deadly

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.360
crisis — all in an official capacity.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.229
And in case anyone thought these were just
rogue agents, China stamped its official seal

00:05:55.229 --> 00:06:01.080
of approval on this behavior in March, when
it held talks with the U.S. in Alaska.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:07.249
There, the most senior Chinese diplomat, who
reports directly to Xi Jinping, pulled no

00:06:07.249 --> 00:06:13.639
punches, saying “The US is not qualified
to speak to China from a position of strength.”

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:18.720
This style of communication has worked about
as well as you’d expect — which is to

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.129
say, very, very poorly.

00:06:21.129 --> 00:06:27.189
The share of Americans who view China unfavorably
reached the highest level on record two years

00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:32.009
ago, and has only continued rising at the
same pace since.

00:06:32.009 --> 00:06:37.310
While this trend may not surprise you, take
a second to appreciate just how overwhelming

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.469
this sentiment is.

00:06:39.469 --> 00:06:44.529
For context, the shift in opinion which has
taken place in the last three years is roughly

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:50.229
equivalent to that in the aftermath of the
1989 Tiananmen Square massacre.

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:55.909
China is now the country Americans view most
negatively — with the exception of only

00:06:55.909 --> 00:06:58.699
Iran and North Korea.

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:05.919
That means Americans view it less favorably
than Russia, Cuba, Iraq, or Afghanistan.

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:12.749
In an unbelievably polarized era, the Communist
Party has truly managed to do the incredible:

00:07:12.749 --> 00:07:15.419
unite Democrats and Republicans.

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:19.780
China is one of the few truly bipartisan issues
in America.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:27.950
A majority of both parties — 77% of conservatives,
54% of liberals, and 56% of moderates, see

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.160
its economic rise as a “critical threat”.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.659
But don’t think this is unique to the U.S.

00:07:35.659 --> 00:07:40.409
Unfavorable views of China are also at their
all-time high in Australia, the UK, Germany,

00:07:40.409 --> 00:07:46.189
the Netherlands, Sweden, South Korea, Spain,
and Canada.

00:07:46.189 --> 00:07:51.930
One of the few countries not to experience
this trend is Japan… but only because it

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.719
had already maxed out.

00:07:53.719 --> 00:07:59.260
Now, it would be both unfair and misleading
to ignore the ways in which the U.S. and other

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:05.479
countries are also responsible for this shift
— in ways both legitimate and not.

00:08:05.479 --> 00:08:11.120
Creating a common, foreign enemy and exploiting
fears of imagined rivals, is, without a doubt,

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.000
a common political strategy for gaining domestic
support.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.250
Innocent Chinese and Asian-Americans have
been caught in the crossfire, used as pawns,

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.030
and accused only of their race.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:28.559
But however much foreign politicians may be
predisposed to portray China as an enemy,

00:08:28.559 --> 00:08:33.430
no government has done more to tarnish its
reputation than China itself.

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:39.099
With ƒcrackdown, sanction, or inflammatory
remark, China makes it harder and harder for

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:41.860
foreign countries to associate with it.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:46.860
Its hostility has brought its southeast Asian
neighbors closer together, made Australia

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:52.050
wary of its influence, and single-handedly
thwarted its investment deal with the European

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.210
Union.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:58.180
Whenever there remains some room for giving
it the benefit of the doubt, China is incredibly

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:01.050
quick to eliminate any ambiguity.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.630
When Huawei tried to prove its independence
from Chinese authorities, for instance, those

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.530
very authorities were the first to defend
the company.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:14.240
In its ongoing crackdown against Jack Ma,
it’s made perfectly clear nobody is above

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.860
the party.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:19.010
The big question, then, is why?

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:24.650
Why are individual diplomats, all the way
up to the highest ranks of power, absolutely

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:28.830
intent on wilfully destroying China’s reputation?

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.580
What does it get out of all this?

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:36.850
To answer this question, we necessarily have
to leave the realm of raw facts and enter

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:38.650
that of speculation.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:44.110
We’ve seen what is — now we try our best
to explain it.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:47.960
The most common theory goes something like
this:

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:55.090
The difference between 2008 and 2018 China
is that today it’s in a position of strength.

00:09:55.090 --> 00:10:01.500
China can afford to be confident and assertive
because a) it’s a large, powerful, and important

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:07.570
country, and b) it genuinely believes in the
decline of its rival, the United States.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:12.890
Its diplomatic approach may be... brash, but
only because it otherwise wouldn’t be taken

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:14.340
seriously.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:19.590
By setting the precedent that China will retaliate
against even the slightest perceived aggression,

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:24.100
smaller, less powerful countries are deterred
from acting out.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:28.960
The problem with this line of thinking is
that strong, powerful nations generally don’t

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.610
go around needlessly destroying their own
reputations.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:37.880
China’s method of “asserting” itself
— calling people “thugs” and “hyenas”

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:39.890
— is just plain cartoonish.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:44.010
It belongs in Hollywood — and that’s no
coincidence.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:49.240
The term used to describe this behavior is
“Wolf Warrior Diplomacy” — a reference

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:54.810
to a hyper-patriotic, Rambo-style Chinese
military film franchise.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:11:00.690
The second theory is that its diplomats are,
in essence, acting — though, the movie only

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.270
makes sense in Chinese.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:08.280
People or countries as confident as China
are always insecure.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.680
Does the kid on the playground who constantly
feels the need to puff up his chest and warn

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.810
you how tough he is, seem confident?

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:20.260
Seasoned diplomats, of course, are not 11-year
olds on the playground.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:25.880
They’re trained professionals with careers
at stake, which suggests their colorful language

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:27.990
serves a real purpose.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:33.470
The reason the smallest dog barks the loudest
is not just to compensate for its relative

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:37.240
size, but also, to convince itself.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:42.860
The audience for the PG-13 rated movie Chinese
diplomats star in every time they tweet an

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:49.190
angry insult is not the world, but rather,
the Chinese public, who respond to these attacks

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:53.830
just as positively as the rest of the world
does negatively.

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:58.800
In response to China’s adversarial tone
in the Alaska talks, for instance, the Chinese

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:03.310
internet was flooded with support for the
diplomats, who were “finally standing up

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:04.860
for the country”.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:06.000
It works.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.560
The language has become more and more inflammatory
because it’s expressly designed to create

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.000
conflict.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.410
The more offended the foreign public, the
more hatred is hurled back at China, which,

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:21.890
in turn, puts the Chinese public on the defensive.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:29.140
It’s a simple trade — lose support abroad,
in exchange for stoking nationalism, and thus

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:30.770
support, at home.

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:35.370
But, is it really worth it?, you may, quite
reasonably, wonder.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:41.320
Credibility is among the most valuable resources
a nation can behold, and rebuilding it takes

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:43.640
years, if not decades.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:49.490
By so thoroughly burning every last bridge,
China has implicitly committed to its aggressive

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.510
tactics for years to come.

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:57.740
With the costs this high, the only world in
which this trade would be worthwhile is if

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:01.560
its leaders were that worried about the outlook
at home.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.040
They may have good reason.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.810
First, its population is rapidly aging.

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:12.970
The past few decades of widespread improvements
to quality of life have coincided with a glut

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:17.950
of productive workers — those aged roughly
30-64.

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:23.880
In the next few decades, these producers will
retire at a staggering rate, and the One-Child

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:28.220
Policy will have left too few people to replace
them.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:32.340
Intense nationalism and the difficulty of
learning the Chinese language, among other

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:37.410
things, will prevent immigrants from filling
in these gaps in any meaningful way.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:42.150
Second, it’s so far failed the transition
to consumption-led growth.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:48.470
Instead, nearly a third of its economy relies
on an unsustainable real estate bubble.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:54.130
The vast majority of new purchases are of
2nd or even 3rd homes, and prices across the

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:58.130
country reflect a highly speculative market.

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:02.860
Because local governments rely on land sales
for their survival, they have no choice but

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:06.480
to encourage wasteful spending merely to balance
the budget.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:12.670
Third, a combination of mismanagement, climate
change, and geographic luck have left China

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:15.250
with a severe water shortage.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:20.700
In the past two decades, 28,000 of its rivers
have dried up, the majority of the rest are

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:25.300
severely polluted, and what remains is only
a drop in the bucket.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:31.050
The North China plain, one of the country’s
most populous regions, faces a Saudi-Arabia-level

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:35.970
crisis, minus the oil riches which enable
desalination.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:41.730
What sets these challenges apart is not their
severity or even scale,

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:46.780
Every country faces obstacles to its development,
and more than three videos could be devoted

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:48.170
to many of them.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:53.720
Neither demographics, housing, nor water problems
are unique to China.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:59.410
In fact, in each case there exists, if not
a solution, at least a way to mitigate the

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:00.980
effects.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:05.070
China could have ended the One-Child Policy
decades earlier,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:10.670
It could accept the short-term losses necessary
to shift its dependence from real estate,

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:15.380
and market-based pricing reform would reduce
its wasteful use of water.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:21.600
No, what makes China unique is that it doesn’t
believe it can admit the extent of these problems,

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:25.050
and therefore can’t adequately address them.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:30.130
In the eyes of the Chinese government, a $100
billion engineering project to physically

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:37.810
move water halfway across the country is easy,
while simply raising water prices is unthinkable.

00:15:37.810 --> 00:15:43.360
The Communist Party has now held power for
as long as the Soviet Union existed, and it’s

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:49.420
acutely aware of how environmental protests
contributed to the downfall of its counterpart.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:54.200
Raising the price of water even slightly would
cause a material change in quality of life

00:15:54.200 --> 00:16:00.110
for millions of poor Chinese, not to mention
the effect it would have on economic growth.

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:05.910
Ecological or pricing concerns may not intuitively
rise to the level of national security, but

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:11.840
in the words of one Canadian researcher, they
do “Present a unifying focal point for dissent

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:16.950
that crosses geographic, cultural, socioeconomic,
and political lines”.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:23.300
While China may project confidence in press
conferences, its actions tell a very different

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:24.300
story.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:28.660
Its military budget receives considerable
attention, but you might be surprised to hear

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:33.260
it actually spends more on domestic security.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:38.530
Provoking nationalism is like training an
attack dog — Sure, you gain a powerful weapon

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:44.420
to use against your real or perceived enemies,
but it's a rather blunt weapon.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:49.650
The more a dog is wound up, the stronger its
bite will be when the time comes to fend off

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:55.040
an attacker, but also the more unstable it
becomes, and the more the owner should be

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.110
worried about losing control.

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:02.870
Nationalism creates immense pressure for the
government to act tough, increasing the cost

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:04.160
of restraint.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:10.250
The one thing it cannot afford to do is be
perceived as weak or indecisive.

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:17.970
Because if it does, it risks becoming the
dog, or rather, wolf’s, next victim.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:23.240
Next year Beijing will once again host the
Olympics, but this time, it won’t be so

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:24.490
welcoming.

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:31.620
If 2008 was the culmination of three decades
of reform and optimism, 2022 is shaping up

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.980
to be just as consequential.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:36.549
What are the odds of a boycott?

00:17:36.549 --> 00:17:40.039
And what will the games signify this time?

